Da Feng Gu Si (大峰古寺) / Nanyang Tong Feng Shan Tang (南洋同奉善堂)
A small single-shrine community temple — quite different in scale from the monasteries previously analyzed, but iconographically rich and structurally illuminating in its own right. The institutional identity, the triadic altar configuration, and the vertical iconographic hierarchy together make this a productive test case for RFIG at the small-temple scale.

Institutional context
The two principal external plaques identify the institution at two layered registers:
南洋同奉善堂  (Nanyang Tong Feng Shan Tang)
  -- Nanyang United Charity Hall
  -- the institution's organizational identity
  -- 善堂 (shàn táng) = "Charity Hall," a specific category
     of religious-charitable society particularly associated
     with the Teochew (Chaozhou) diaspora community across
     Southeast Asia
  -- Charity Hall societies traditionally provide:
       * burial rites for the destitute
       * medical relief
       * disaster aid
       * food distribution to the poor
       * moral cultivation and worship

大峰古寺  (Dafeng Gu Si)
  -- Ancient Temple of Master Dafeng
  -- the temple's iconographic-devotional identity
  -- named after Master Dafeng (大峰祖師), the 12th-century
     Song-dynasty Teochew monk whose charitable foundations
     became the model for the Shan Tang tradition across
     the Chinese diaspora
The dual naming encodes a dual identity: the institution is simultaneously a charitable society (Shan Tang) and a temple (Si) — operating as religious-charitable hybrid characteristic of Teochew diaspora institutional life. This is a different categorical register from the Buddhist monasteries analyzed earlier. The Shan Tang tradition operates at the intersection of Buddhist devotion (Master Dafeng's monastic origins), Daoist iconography (auxiliary deities), Confucian moral cultivation (charitable practice as virtue), and folk-religious community formation (diaspora identity maintenance).

